Verdict: MAJOR  
Retrieval adequacy: insufficient

Findings:
- [major] The plan is built on an unverified control-plane topology and misses an active repo/contract mismatch that directly affects this issue’s boundary work. `docs/standards/CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md` says provider config lives under dedicated directories and explicitly lists `.claude/CLAUDE.md` as required, but the live repo has root [`CLAUDE.md`](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/CLAUDE.md) and no `.claude/CLAUDE.md` at all. The plan’s “Resource Intelligence Summary” and pseudocode never surface this mismatch, yet the issue requires “contract boundaries relative to `CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md`” and “discoverability anchors from existing control-plane entrypoints.” Any readiness contract written without first reconciling or explicitly documenting this discrepancy is likely to encode the wrong entrypoint model.
- [major] The anchor strategy is internally inconsistent with the current provider surfaces. The plan says in Pseudocode and Risks that discoverability will be added only to `AGENTS.md` and `docs/standards/CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md` while “provider adapters remain thin and unchanged.” But live [`GEMINI.md`](https://github.com/vamseeachanta/workspace-hub/blob/main/GEMINI.md) already points users to `docs/work-queue-workflow.md`, `docs/modules/ai/AGENT_EQUIVALENCE_ARCHITECTURE.md`, `docs/governance/SESSION-GOVERNANCE.md`, and `.gemini/`. Leaving adapters unchanged preserves conflicting discoverability anchors after this issue claims to define canonical workspace-hub entrypoints. That is a scope/correctness defect, not a style preference.
- [major] The verification plan is not executable in the form described. The TDD list includes `test_non_contradiction_with_control_plane_contract`, `test_anchor_strategy_matches_control_plane_contract`, and `test_scope_is_workspace_hub_only`, with expected outputs like “no contradiction markers” and “explicit consistent references only.” Those are not machine-checkable oracles unless the plan first defines a schema or deterministic rule set for contradictions, canonical anchors, and scope boundaries. Nothing in `CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md` provides such a schema. As written, these tests will collapse into brittle substring assertions and provide false confidence.
- [major] The plan invents a “thin-adapter line-count compliance” requirement that is not supported by the issue or by `docs/standards/CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md`. The TDD list and Acceptance Criteria add `test_line_count_compliance_for_thin_adapters` and require “line-count compliance for thin adapters,” but the control-plane contract only says adapters “may add provider-specific detail” and “can be minimal/empty”; it does not define a line limit. Worse, the proposed test input includes `AGENTS.md`, which the same contract defines as the canonical repo entry point, not a thin adapter. This adds non-issue policy drift and likely rework.
- [minor] `docs/plans/README.md` is listed under “Files to Change” as if it were part of the implementation deliverable, but that file is already the planning index and is required to be updated when the plan is created. Folding plan-index bookkeeping into the issue implementation muddies the deliverable set without helping the acceptance criteria.

Missing tests:
- A test that verifies all claimed canonical discoverability anchors are consistent across the actual live entrypoints, including `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `GEMINI.md`, and `CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md`.
- A test or explicit audit step for the root-vs-hidden Claude adapter location mismatch (`CLAUDE.md` vs `.claude/CLAUDE.md`) before writing any new contract language.
- A test that ensures the new contract/playbook does not point to stale Gemini-specific workflow docs that contradict the chosen canonical anchor model.

Scope creep concerns:
- “line-count compliance for thin adapters” is extra policy not requested by issue #2408.
- Updating `docs/plans/README.md` as an implementation deliverable is planning-admin work, not release-readiness contract work.
- The report package at `docs/reports/2026-04-20-issue-2408-workspace-hub-readiness-package.md` risks becoming broader than the issue’s requested “concise workspace-hub-only gap summary.”

Weakest assumption:
- The assumption that `AGENTS.md` plus `CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md` can become the sole anchor layer while provider adapters remain unchanged.

Most likely implementation failure mode:
- The docs land with a “canonical” anchor story that still disagrees with the live provider entrypoints, especially Gemini and the Claude root-vs-hidden adapter split, so the contract immediately ships with discoverability drift.

Most likely test gap:
- Tests pass on keyword presence while missing real cross-file inconsistency between the new standards docs and the live adapter files.

Future issues suggested:
- Reconcile the actual workspace-hub provider adapter topology with `CONTROL_PLANE_CONTRACT.md` before or alongside any model-release-readiness contract.
- Normalize or explicitly document provider-specific discoverability anchors so `AGENTS.md`, `CLAUDE.md`, `GEMINI.md`, and the control-plane contract do not compete.

Review confidence: high
